(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["local-regulation"]="<h1>Local Control of Blood Flow</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-to-control-its-own-blood-flow\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why does a tissue need to control its own blood flow?</h2>\n<p>The heart sets the total output, but each tissue needs a supply matched to <strong>its own</strong> needs, moment to moment. A sprinting leg muscle needs far more blood than it did at rest. The brain and kidneys need a <strong>steady</strong> supply, even when blood pressure wobbles.</p>\n<p>Recall: <strong>flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance</strong> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=2-what-actually-determines-flow\">→ Haemodynamics, §2</a>)</em>. A tissue can't change the arterial pressure, but it <strong>can</strong> change its own resistance, by widening or narrowing its <strong>arterioles</strong>. And because resistance depends on the <strong>fourth power of the radius</strong>, a small change in arteriolar width has a big effect on flow: halving the radius raises the resistance about <strong>16 times</strong>.</p>\n<p>Local control comes in two forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"autoregulation\">Autoregulation</span>:</strong> keeping flow <strong>steady</strong> when the pressure changes (§2–3).</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperaemia:</strong> <strong>raising</strong> flow when the tissue needs more (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation?section=4-active-and-reactive-hyperaemia\">§4</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"2-autoregulation-steady-flow-despite-changing-pressure\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Autoregulation: steady flow despite changing pressure</h2>\n<p><strong>Autoregulation</strong> is a tissue's ability to keep its blood flow <strong>relatively constant</strong> when its perfusion pressure changes, by adjusting its own resistance.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>If perfusion pressure…</th>\n  <th>Flow at first…</th>\n  <th>The arterioles…</th>\n  <th>So flow…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Rises</strong></td>\n  <td>Rises</td>\n  <td><strong>Constrict</strong> (resistance up)</td>\n  <td>Returns towards normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Falls</strong></td>\n  <td>Falls</td>\n  <td><strong>Dilate</strong> (resistance down)</td>\n  <td>Returns towards normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>It is strongest in the <strong>brain</strong>, <strong>kidneys</strong> and <strong>heart</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=3-how-does-the-brain-keep-its-blood-flow-steady\">→ Cerebral Circulation, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>It only works within a range.</strong> If pressure falls so far that the arterioles are already fully dilated, they have nothing left to give, and flow falls with pressure. This is why severe shock damages the brain and kidneys despite autoregulation. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>How does a tissue do it?</strong> The lecture gives two theories. They are not rivals: both work at the same time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-two-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Two mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic</h2>\n<h3>The myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Pressure rises, so the arteriole is <strong>stretched</strong>.</li>\n<li>Stretch opens channels in the smooth muscle, which <strong>contracts</strong>.</li>\n<li>The arteriole <strong>narrows</strong>, raising resistance, so flow comes back down.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When pressure falls, the opposite happens: less stretch, the muscle relaxes, and the arteriole widens.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 140\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Higher transmural pressure stretches an arteriole, triggering smooth-muscle contraction, raising resistance and opposing a large rise in flow\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e9f3fd\" stroke=\"#4d84aa\"/><rect x=\"224\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e4f4ea\" stroke=\"#4e9b74\"/><rect x=\"443\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2d6\" stroke=\"#b88933\"/><g fill=\"#20324b\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"91\" y=\"57\">Pressure ↑</text><text x=\"91\" y=\"77\">Wall stretch ↑</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"57\">Smooth muscle</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"77\">contracts</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"57\">Radius ↓; R ↑</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"77\">Flow rise opposed</text></g><path d=\"M181 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7M400 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7\" stroke=\"#527895\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></svg><p>Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why is this protective?</strong> It stops a surge of pressure from being passed straight into the fragile capillaries downstream.</p>\n<p><strong>The law of Laplace: why small vessels don't burst.</strong> For a cylindrical vessel, the <strong><span data-term=\"wall-tension\">wall tension</span></strong> needed to resist the pressure inside is:</p>\n<p><strong>T = P × r</strong> (wall tension = transmural pressure × radius)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transmural pressure</strong> = pressure inside − pressure in the surrounding tissue.</li>\n<li>At the same pressure, a vessel with <strong>half the radius</strong> needs only <strong>half the wall tension</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So a capillary, with its tiny radius, can withstand blood pressure with a wall only one cell thick. And a vessel that <strong>widens</strong> (as in an aneurysm) needs <strong>more</strong> tension at the same pressure, so it is more likely to stretch further and rupture.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture writes the general form as P = T(1/R₁ + 1/R₂), which for a cylinder simplifies to P = T/r, i.e. T = P × r. Its other form, P = T/(R₁ + R₂), is not equivalent and has been left out.</aside>\n\n<h3>The metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs</h3>\n<p>When a tissue's blood supply falls short of its needs, its <strong>metabolites build up</strong>. These <strong>dilate</strong> the arterioles, so flow rises and <strong>washes the metabolites away</strong>. As they clear, the arterioles narrow again. It is a self-correcting loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Which signals dilate?</strong> Think about what a tissue short of blood would produce:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low O₂</strong></li>\n<li><strong>High CO₂</strong> and <strong>H⁺</strong> (low pH), including <strong>lactic acid</strong></li>\n<li><strong>K⁺</strong>, released by active cells</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"adenosine\">Adenosine</span></strong> (and ADP in heart muscle), from ATP breakdown</li>\n<li><strong>Higher temperature</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Which signals constrict?</strong> <strong>Serotonin</strong> (from platelets, at an injured vessel), <strong>cold</strong> and <strong>angiotensin II</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Myogenic or metabolic: what does each sense?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myogenic</strong> senses <strong>pressure</strong> (stretch of the vessel wall).</li>\n<li><strong>Metabolic</strong> senses <strong>supply versus demand</strong> in the tissue.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-active-and-reactive-hyperaemia\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Active and reactive hyperaemia</h2>\n<p><strong>Hyperaemia</strong> simply means <strong>increased blood flow</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong><span data-term=\"active-hyperaemia\">Active hyperaemia</span></strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>When</td>\n  <td><strong>During</strong> increased activity</td>\n  <td><strong>After</strong> blood flow was cut off for a while</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Example</td>\n  <td>Working muscle in exercise</td>\n  <td>The red flush of a hand after a tight cuff is released</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Why</td>\n  <td>Active cells make more metabolites, which dilate the arterioles</td>\n  <td>During the blockage, metabolites built up and the arterioles dilated; when flow returns, it <strong>surges</strong> into the wide-open bed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Then</td>\n  <td>Flow stays high as long as the activity continues</td>\n  <td>Flow overshoots, washes the metabolites out, then returns to normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The longer the blockage, the bigger and longer the reactive surge: the tissue is &quot;repaying&quot; its oxygen debt.</p>\n<p><strong>Don't confuse these with autoregulation.</strong> Autoregulation keeps flow <strong>steady</strong> when pressure changes. Hyperaemia <strong>raises</strong> flow because the tissue needs it.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–4\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–4 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Perfusion pressure to a kidney rises suddenly. How does autoregulation respond?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The arterioles dilate, lowering resistance so flow rises further</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The heart slows, lowering the pressure that reaches the kidney</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The arterioles constrict, raising resistance so flow returns towards normal</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The kidney's veins constrict, holding more blood in the organ</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"local-regulation:ccbfb09a\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> More pressure stretches the arterioles, which constrict. The higher resistance brings flow back down.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why can a capillary withstand blood pressure with a wall only one cell thick?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Its blood pressure is higher than in the larger arteries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its tiny radius means only a small wall tension is needed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its wall contains thick smooth muscle that resists stretch</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Its large radius spreads the pressure over a bigger area</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"local-regulation:8f808464\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> By the law of Laplace, T = P × r. A very small radius needs very little tension.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A tourniquet is removed after five minutes, and the limb flushes red. What explains this?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The muscles in the limb began to work harder after release</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The rise in arterial pressure stretched and opened the arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Sympathetic nerves to the limb were activated by the tourniquet</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Metabolites built up during the blockage, dilating the arterioles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"local-regulation:587706ad\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> This is reactive hyperaemia: the dilated bed receives a surge of blood when flow returns.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"5-signals-from-the-endothelium\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Signals from the endothelium</h2>\n<p>The <strong>endothelium</strong>, the single-cell lining of the vessels, is not just a passive tube. It releases signals that act on the smooth muscle underneath:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Signal</th>\n  <th>Effect</th>\n  <th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Nitric oxide (NO)</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Dilates</strong></td>\n  <td>Released when flowing blood drags on the endothelium (<strong>shear stress</strong>). It diffuses into the smooth muscle, raises <strong>cGMP</strong>, and the muscle relaxes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Prostacyclin</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Dilates</strong></td>\n  <td>Also stops platelets clumping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Endothelin-1</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Constricts</strong></td>\n  <td>A powerful vasoconstrictor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Several other substances dilate vessels <strong>through the endothelium</strong>, by making it release NO: <strong>bradykinin</strong>, <strong><span data-term=\"substance-p\">substance P</span></strong> and <strong>acetylcholine</strong>. <strong>Histamine</strong> (via H₂ receptors) and <strong><span data-term=\"anp\">ANP</span></strong> also dilate vessels. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/skin?section=3-the-triple-response-of-lewis\">→ Cutaneous Circulation, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why does this matter?</strong> When the endothelium is damaged (in atherosclerosis, smoking or diabetes), it makes less NO. The vessels dilate less well, and the balance tips towards constriction and clotting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-local-control-and-the-whole-body\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Local control and the whole body</h2>\n<p>Local control works <strong>alongside</strong> the nerves and hormones of the next two chapters. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/neural-humoral\">→ Neural and Hormonal Control</a>)</em> Sometimes they cooperate; sometimes they compete:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In exercise</strong>, sympathetic nerves constrict arterioles throughout the body. But in the working muscles, metabolites dilate them so strongly that they <strong>override</strong> the sympathetic signal. So blood is <strong>redirected</strong> to where it is needed.</li>\n<li><strong>In haemorrhage</strong>, sympathetic constriction diverts blood from the skin and gut. If a tissue becomes badly starved of blood, its metabolites start to fight back, dilating its arterioles.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 5–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 5–6 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 5–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> How does nitric oxide relax vascular smooth muscle?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It raises cGMP in the smooth muscle, which relaxes it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It blocks sympathetic nerves supplying the arteriole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It raises calcium in the smooth muscle, which relaxes it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It lowers the blood pressure inside the arteriole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"local-regulation:92b0f3e8\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> NO diffuses from the endothelium into the smooth muscle, activates guanylyl cyclase and raises cGMP.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> During exercise, sympathetic activity rises, yet blood flow to the working muscles increases. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Sympathetic nerves do not supply the arterioles in skeletal muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The rise in blood pressure alone stretches open the muscle arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Myogenic constriction is switched off in skeletal muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Local metabolites dilate the muscle arterioles, overriding the constriction</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"local-regulation:c7a09c40\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Metabolic dilation in active muscle beats sympathetic constriction, so blood is redirected to the muscle.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which of these constricts arterioles locally?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Adenosine released from active cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Serotonin released from platelets</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Potassium released from active cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Nitric oxide released from endothelium</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"local-regulation:327f8f50\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Serotonin, cold and angiotensin II constrict. Adenosine, K⁺ and NO all dilate.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Flow = pressure difference ÷ resistance.</strong> Tissues control their flow by changing <strong>arteriolar radius</strong> (resistance ∝ 1/r⁴).</li>\n<li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> keeps flow steady when pressure changes, within a range. Strongest in the brain, kidneys and heart.</li>\n<li><strong>Myogenic:</strong> stretch → smooth muscle contracts → arteriole narrows. <strong>Senses pressure.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Metabolic:</strong> low O₂, high CO₂/H⁺, lactic acid, K⁺, adenosine, heat → dilation → metabolites washed out. <strong>Senses supply versus demand.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Local constrictors:</strong> serotonin, cold, angiotensin II.</li>\n<li><strong>Law of Laplace (cylinder): T = P × r.</strong> Small vessels need little wall tension; widened vessels need more.</li>\n<li><strong>Active hyperaemia:</strong> more flow during activity. <strong>Reactive hyperaemia:</strong> a surge after a blockage.</li>\n<li><strong>Endothelium:</strong> NO (via cGMP) and prostacyclin dilate; endothelin-1 constricts. Bradykinin, substance P and ACh dilate through NO.</li>\n<li><strong>In exercise</strong>, local metabolic dilation overrides sympathetic constriction in working muscle.</li>\n</ol>\n";
